          %title Wheels
          %h1#wheels Wheels
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#notation"} Notation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A wheel is an algebraic concept similar to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            but in which division is
            %em always
            defined, even division by zero. This naturally requires modifications to the usual axioms. Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            gives rise to a wheel using a simple modification of the construction of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+of+fractions"} field of fractions
          %p
            The terminology ‘wheel’ comes from the example of the wheel of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+real+numbers"} extended real numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
          %h2#notation Notation
          %p
            In elementary algebra, it's traditional to write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -x
            for the opposite (or additive inverse) of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 - x
            (or perhaps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1x
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1 \cdot x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y - x = y + (-x)
            %em not
            common to write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x
            for the reciprocal (or multiplicative inverse) of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/x
            (or perhaps
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{-1}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y/x = y \cdot /x
          %p
            In a wheel, the unary operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            is a fundamental part of the structure, and the binary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            is only defined using it. Although it remains true that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y/x = y \cdot /x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/x
            instead of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{-1}
            instead of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x
            is always the multiplicative inverse of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in a wheel. (But for that matter,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -x
            is not always the additive inverse of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in a wheel, even though we can still write it, and indeed define it, as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1 \cdot x
          %p
            If we write multiplication as juxtaposition, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y /x
            is naturally interpreted as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \cdot /x
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y /x
            as always meaning
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \cdot /x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            really matters. But notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x y
            means
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x \cdot y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /(x y)
            (which is different, and in fact equal to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x \cdot /y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /x /y
            for short). So we are coming down on the side of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 16
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 8 /2 (2 + 2) = 16
            in the great PEMDAS debate.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong wheel
            consists of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            equipped with two binary operations written with any common notation for addition and multiplication, a unary operation written with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /
            as a prefix (called the reciprocal), and two constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %ul
            %li
              Addition and multiplication are each associative and commutative, with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              (respectively) as identities;
            %li
              The reciprocal is an
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} //x = x
            %li
              Reciprocals get along with multiplication:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /(x y) = /x /y
              and
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /1 = 1
            %li
              Multiplication sort of distributes over addition, but not quite: neither
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 x
              nor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x + y) z
              can be simplified in general, but instead we have
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x + y) z + 0 z = x z + y z
            %li
              An axiom that would imply that reciprocals are multiplicative inverses if distributivity held:
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x + y z) /z = x / z + y + 0 z
            %li
              Special properties of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              that are needed since distributivity doesn't hold:
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 0 = 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x + 0 y) z = x z + 0 y
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /(x + 0 y) = /x + 0 y
            %li
              Although
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 /0
              doesn't simplify, it's an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absorbing+element"} absorbing element
              for addition:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 0
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 /0 + x = 0 /0
          %p
            Although these rules are somewhat odd, we need some unusual behaviour to prevent a wheel from collapsing to a triviality via
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 0
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = 0 x = 0 /0 x = 1 x = x
          %p We can generalize the quasi-distributive rule beyond two terms as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\sum_{i=1}^n x_i) y + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (0 y) = \sum_{i=1}^n (x_i y) .
          %p
            That is, to multiply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            by the sum of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            terms, you need to add in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 y
            as well, and then the result is the sum of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            products.
          %p
            Wheels are like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigs"} rigs
            in that there is generally no notion of subtraction. However, if the wheel happens to have a (necessarily unique) additive inverse of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -x
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1 x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y - x
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y + -x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -x
            is
            %em not
            in general an additive inverse of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mn 0
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x - x = 0 x + 0 x
            (using the modified general form of distributivity).
          %p
            It is common to write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} /0
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \infty
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The original example is the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong wheel of real numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\odot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ⊙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\odot
            is an equivalence class of pairs of real numbers under a certain equivalence relation. Morally,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c,d)
            are set equivalent iff
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a d = b c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0)
            equivalent to every other pair (and so it's not even transitive). So we additionally require that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≠
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b) \ne (0,0)
            iff
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c,d) \ne (0,0)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0)
            is actually equivalent only to itself. We may write the equivalence class of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a/b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a : b
            instead until we've established what
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a /b
            means in
            = succeed "." do
